== 2.7 Chapter 2 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [The law of demand states that, other things being equal,],
  [demand rises as income rises],
  [price and quantity demanded move in the same direction],
  [price and quantity demanded move in opposite directions],
  [supply creates its own demand]
)

#mcq(2, [Demand in economics means the quantity of a good that buyers],
  [are willing and able to buy at each price over a period],
  [actually bought last year],
  [would like to have at any price],
  [can afford at the current price only]
)

#mcq(3, [A demand schedule is],
  [a table showing quantities demanded at different prices],
  [a record of past purchases],
  [a graph of demand against supply],
  [a list of buyers in a market]
)

#mcq(4, [At ₦10 the quantity demanded is 20 units; at ₦8 it is 30 units. The demand curve therefore],
  [slopes upward],
  [slopes downward],
  [is vertical],
  [shifts to the right as price falls]
)

#mcq(5, [A movement along a demand curve is caused by a change in],
  [the price of a substitute],
  [the price of the good itself],
  [tastes and fashion],
  [consumers' income]
)

#mcq(6, [A shift of the demand curve to the right is called],
  [an increase in demand],
  [an expansion of demand],
  [a contraction of demand],
  [a decrease in quantity demanded]
)

#mcq(7, [Which of the following will shift the demand curve for a normal good to the left?],
  [a rise in consumers' income],
  [a fall in consumers' income],
  [a fall in its price],
  [a rise in its price]
)

#mcq(8, [If the price of coffee rises, the demand curve for tea is likely to],
  [remain unchanged],
  [shift to the left],
  [shift to the right],
  [become vertical]
)

#mcq(9, [If the price of petrol rises, the demand curve for cars is likely to],
  [slope upward],
  [shift to the right],
  [remain unchanged],
  [shift to the left]
)

#mcq(10, [Two goods are complements if],
  [an increase in the price of one raises the demand for the other],
  [they are produced by the same firm],
  [they have the same price],
  [they are used together, so an increase in the price of one reduces demand for the other]
)

#mcq(11, [An inferior good is one for which],
  [the demand curve slopes upward],
  [demand rises as income rises],
  [demand does not respond to price],
  [demand falls as income rises]
)

#mcq(12, [An increase in population will],
  [shift the supply curve to the right],
  [shift the demand curve for most goods to the right],
  [have no effect on demand],
  [move consumers along the demand curve]
)

#mcq(13, [If buyers expect the price of rice to rise sharply next month, the demand for rice today will],
  [fall],
  [stay the same],
  [rise],
  [become perfectly inelastic]
)

#mcq(14, [The demand curve for a Giffen good],
  [is horizontal],
  [is vertical],
  [slopes downward like any other],
  [slopes upward over part of its range]
)

#mcq(15, [Goods bought mainly because they are expensive are called],
  [Giffen goods],
  [goods of ostentation],
  [inferior goods],
  [complementary goods]
)

#mcq(16, [Utility in economics means],
  [the usefulness of a good to society],
  [the total value of goods consumed],
  [the satisfaction derived from consuming a good],
  [the price a consumer is willing to pay]
)

#mcq(17, [Total utility is at a maximum when marginal utility is],
  [zero],
  [positive],
  [at its maximum],
  [negative]
)

#mcq(18, [Marginal utility is],
  [the same as average utility],
  [the change in total utility from consuming one more unit],
  [total utility divided by the number of units],
  [the satisfaction from the first unit only]
)

#mcq(19, [The law of diminishing marginal utility states that],
  [consumers always prefer more to less],
  [total utility falls as consumption rises],
  [the satisfaction from each additional unit falls as more is consumed in a period],
  [utility cannot be measured]
)

#mcq(20, [A consumer's total utility from 1, 2 and 3 units is 8, 14 and 18. The marginal utility of the second unit is],
  [14],
  [8],
  [4],
  [6]
)

#mcq(21, [If the marginal utility of a fourth unit is negative, total utility],
  [is rising at an increasing rate],
  [is at its maximum],
  [is zero],
  [falls when the fourth unit is consumed]
)

#mcq(22, [A consumer is in equilibrium when],
  [average utility is at its maximum],
  [marginal utility is zero for every good],
  [total utility is at its maximum for one good],
  [marginal utility per naira is equal for every good bought]
)

#mcq(23, [MU of X is 20 and its price is ₦4; MU of Y is 15 and its price is ₦5. The consumer should],
  [keep the present combination],
  [buy more Y and less X],
  [buy more X and less Y],
  [buy more of both]
)

#mcq(24, [The demand curve can be derived from utility analysis because a consumer buys an extra unit only when],
  [the good is a necessity],
  [the price equals the total utility of all units],
  [the good has no substitutes],
  [the price equals the marginal utility of that unit measured in money]
)

#mcq(25, [If demand for a good increases while its supply is unchanged, the result is],
  [a fall in price],
  [no change in price],
  [a fall in quantity],
  [a rise in price]
)

#mcq(26, [Which of the following is NOT a determinant of demand?],
  [the price of the good itself],
  [the price of related goods],
  [consumers' income],
  [population]
)

#mcq(27, [An increase in the price of a good causes],
  [a decrease in quantity demanded],
  [a shift of the demand curve to the left],
  [an increase in demand],
  [a decrease in demand]
)

#mcq(28, [A normal good is one for which],
  [demand rises as income rises],
  [the demand curve slopes upward],
  [demand falls as income rises],
  [demand is unrelated to income]
)

#mcq(29, [The demand curve for salt is likely to be],
  [upward-sloping],
  [inelastic, because salt has few substitutes and takes a small share of income],
  [elastic, because salt is widely used],
  [perfectly elastic]
)

#mcq(30, [If a 5% rise in the price of a good reduces quantity demanded by 10%, demand is],
  [inelastic],
  [unit elastic],
  [elastic],
  [perfectly inelastic]
)

#mcq(31, [The market demand curve is obtained by],
  [averaging the prices of all consumers],
  [multiplying price by quantity],
  [adding the quantities demanded by all consumers at each price],
  [adding the incomes of all consumers]
)

#mcq(32, [A movement from a point inside the demand curve to a point on it, after a price fall, is called],
  [a decrease in demand],
  [an expansion of demand],
  [a shift of demand],
  [an increase in demand]
)

#mcq(33, [Which of the following best explains the downward slope of the demand curve?],
  [the law of diminishing returns],
  [the law of supply],
  [the law of diminishing marginal utility, together with the substitution and income effects],
  [increasing returns to scale]
)

#mcq(34, [If the price of a good falls and, at the same time, consumers' incomes rise, the quantity demanded will],
  [certainly fall],
  [be indeterminate],
  [remain unchanged],
  [certainly rise, because both changes push it up]
)

#mcq(35, [A demand function Q = 100 − 4P gives a quantity demanded of 60 when the price is],
  [₦5],
  [₦40],
  [₦15],
  [₦10]
)

#mcq(36, [The difference between demand and effective demand is that effective demand],
  [is measured in units rather than naira],
  [refers only to luxury goods],
  [is backed by the ability to pay],
  [exists only in a command economy]
)

#mcq(37, [Utility analysis assumes that consumers],
  [act rationally to maximise satisfaction within their budgets],
  [always buy the cheapest good],
  [have unlimited income],
  [cannot compare different goods]
)

#mcq(38, [If a good is a necessity, its demand curve is likely to be],
  [horizontal],
  [vertical],
  [relatively steep],
  [relatively flat]
)

#mcq(39, [A consumer spends ₦200 on two goods. If the price of one good rises, the budget line],
  [is unaffected],
  [shifts inward],
  [shifts outward],
  [pivots inward along the axis of the good whose price rose]
)

#mcq(40, [When the price of a good falls, consumers can afford to buy more of it because their real income has risen. This is called],
  [the income effect],
  [the demonstration effect],
  [the law of diminishing returns],
  [the substitution effect]
)

