  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Opposite directions. The other three are the law of supply, the income effect and Say's law.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Willing and able, at each price, over a period. A wish without purchasing power is not demand.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    A table of price and quantity demanded. The graph drawn from it is the demand curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Lower price, larger quantity: the curve slopes downward. Nothing has shifted, because only the price changed.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    The good's own price moves the consumer along the curve. The other three shift the whole curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    An increase in demand — a larger quantity at every price. An expansion is a movement along the curve caused by a price fall.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Lower income reduces demand for a normal good. A price change, in either direction, is a movement along the curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Tea is a substitute, so dearer coffee sends buyers towards tea and its demand shifts right.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Cars and petrol are complements, so a dearer complement reduces the demand for the car at every price.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Used together. The first option defines substitutes, which is why the two definitions are so often swapped in the options.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Demand falls as income rises. It is a statement about the relationship, not about the quality of the good.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    More buyers mean more demanded at every price. Population is a determinant, so the curve moves.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Expectation of a future rise brings buying forward, shifting today's demand right.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Upward over part of its range: a price rise can raise quantity demanded because the good absorbs so much of a poor household's budget.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Veblen or ostentatious goods, where the price is part of the appeal. A Giffen good is bought more when it is dearer for a different reason.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Satisfaction, which is subjective. Usefulness to society is a different idea, and the paper sets it as the trap.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Zero. A positive marginal adds to the total and a negative one subtracts, so the turning point is the zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    The change in the total from one more unit. The average is the total divided by the number of units.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Each additional unit adds less. Total utility can still be rising while marginal utility falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    14 − 8 = 6. The 14 is total utility and the 8 is the marginal utility of the first unit.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    It falls. A negative marginal utility subtracts from the total, which is the definition of the third stage of consumption.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Equal marginal utility per naira, with the whole income spent. Equal *total* utility is not required and equal marginal utility alone is not enough when prices differ.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    X returns 5 utils per naira and Y returns 3, so spending should move towards X until the two ratios are equal.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    The consumer equates price with marginal utility in money terms, and since marginal utility falls, a lower price is needed for each extra unit.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    A rightward shift of demand along an unchanged supply curve raises the equilibrium price.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    The good's own price is not a determinant of demand; it determines the *quantity* demanded. The word NOT is the whole question.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    A decrease in quantity demanded — a movement along the curve. *Demand* is the whole curve and only a determinant shifts it.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Positive income elasticity. The opposite relationship defines an inferior good.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Inelastic. Few substitutes and a tiny share of the budget are the two strongest reasons for unresponsiveness.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    10 ÷ 5 = 2, which is greater than one, so demand is elastic.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Horizontal summation: at each price, add the quantities. Multiplying price by quantity gives total revenue.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Expansion — a movement along the curve caused by a price fall. The curve itself has not moved.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Diminishing marginal utility plus the substitution and income effects. Diminishing returns explains the supply curve, not demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Both changes push quantity demanded up, so the direction is certain even if the size is not.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    60 = 100 − 4P, so 4P = 40 and P = ₦10. Substituting each option is the quickest check.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Purchasing power. A household that wants a good but cannot afford it creates no effective demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Rational maximisation subject to a budget. Assuming unlimited income would remove the budget constraint that makes the equilibrium condition necessary.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    Steep, meaning inelastic: quantity barely changes when price changes. A vertical curve would be perfectly inelastic, which is an extreme.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    The same income now buys less of that good, so the intercept on its axis falls and the line pivots.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.03pt)[
    The income effect: the same money income now buys more. The substitution effect is the separate pull towards the good that has become relatively cheaper.
  ]

]

